            🌰 Historical Heritage: Why Zanzibar Became the "Spice Island"
          </h4>
          <p style="color: #78350f; font-size: 0.88rem; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;">
            In the 1830s, Sultan Seyyid Said moved his royal court from Muscat to Stone Town, realizing that Zanzibar's humid equatorial climate and fertile volcanic soil provided the world's most ideal conditions for growing spices. Within decades, Zanzibar supplied over 70% of the globe's cloves. Today, village farming cooperatives preserve traditional organic cultivation methods without chemical pesticides, supporting local farm livelihoods.
          </p>
